WEBVTT

00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:02.402
This slide summarizes the normalization progression.

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:07.852
First Normal Form removes repeating groups and list-like values so each field represents one value.

00:00:07.852 --> 00:00:14.302
Second Normal Form handles partial dependencies when a composite key is involved.

00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:15.942
Facts that depend on only part of that key move elsewhere.

00:00:15.942 --> 00:00:21.282
Third Normal Form removes transitive dependencies so non-key facts are stored with the entity that actually

00:00:21.282 --> 00:00:27.512
determines them. A fast diagnostic is to ask how many rows must change when one fact changes.

00:00:27.512 --> 00:00:31.877
If changing a patient's name requires updating many appointment-service rows,

00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:35.652
patient information is probably stored in the wrong place.

00:00:35.652 --> 00:00:40.842
In a well-designed operational schema, one patient fact should usually be updated in one patient row.

00:00:40.842 --> 00:00:44.102
Normalization is easier to understand as anomaly prevention.

00:00:44.102 --> 00:00:47.667
Repeating facts in many rows makes updates inconsistent, inserts awkward, and deletes risky.

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:51.907
Splitting data into well-defined entities reduces that repetition.

00:00:51.907 --> 00:00:57.920
The goal is not to create the maximum number of tables; it is to store each fact in a defensible place.
